  "narrative": "All twelve new discovery results are valid Welch estimates with adequate cells and are interpreted on the registered signed scale. For the negative-direction PTEN-loss and CDKN2A-loss claims, a positive signed estimate means lower dependency_composite and therefore stronger dependency. For the positive-direction technical Avana-library claim, a positive signed estimate means higher dependency_composite and therefore weaker dependency.\n\nThe PTEN-loss context set is partially informative. H159 is the cleanest new PTEN-loss refinement: among TP53-loss, KEAP1-loss, and SMARCA4-loss negative cells, PTEN loss is associated with lower dependency_composite by +0.252 [0.169, 0.335], with the 95% lower bound above the 0.15 threshold and adequate cells. I accept this as a dataset-specific association and request independent validation because it is a borderline-accepted refinement of the precision-limited PTEN central association. H165 also clears threshold in lower-half screen-roc_auc cells, +0.363 [0.192, 0.535]; I accept it as a context-supporting association, but I close it rather than validate it because the overall PTEN association is already centrally validated and conditioning on lower screen quality may reflect measurement context, batch selection, or collider-like dependence rather than a generalizable biology.\n\nThe other PTEN-quality or co-genotype-free high-quality contexts remain unresolved. H164 broad upper screen quality is +0.192 [0.082, 0.303], H166 matched RNA+DNA QC is +0.242 [0.138, 0.346], and H167 triple-negative upper screen quality is +0.201 [0.108, 0.293]. These have positive point estimates above or near threshold but lower bounds below 0.15, so they do not establish threshold-supported context-specific associations. I defer them as precision/context questions, not evidence of absence. The apparent attenuation among some higher-quality or QC-selected PTEN contexts may reflect sample selection, confounding, or reduced exposure counts rather than a true loss of PTEN association.\n\nCDKN2A loss remains unresolved and below threshold. The new cleaner contexts are directionally positive but none has a signed lower bound above 0.15. H162 triple-negative CDKN2A is +0.144 [0.083, 0.204], below threshold despite large cells. H170 upper screen quality is +0.144 [0.056, 0.231]. H168 TP53-loss positive upper screen quality is +0.212 [0.053, 0.370], imprecise and not threshold-established. H169 TP53-loss negative upper screen quality is +0.142 [0.053, 0.230], below threshold. H171 triple-negative upper screen quality is +0.159 [0.086, 0.232], point estimate just above threshold but lower bound below threshold. H163 highest screen quality crosses zero, +0.060 [-0.099, 0.219]. These results suggest that any CDKN2A-loss association is smaller and less stable than the five central predictors, and I defer further CDKN2A work rather than reject it because several intervals remain compatible with threshold-level effects.\n\nThe technical Avana-library comparison H174 is rejected: +0.053 [-0.035, 0.141], with the entire interval below the 0.15 threshold and crossing zero. This provides no evidence for a scientifically meaningful Avana-library-associated shift in dependency_composite in the registered direction.\n\nOverall, the accepted central dataset map remains unchanged and validated: TP53 loss and KEAP1 loss are associated with higher dependency_composite values, i.e. weaker dependency, while PTEN loss, SMARCA4 loss, and MSI-high are associated with lower dependency_composite values, i.e. stronger dependency. The new accepted PTEN contexts are robustness-supporting refinements, not independent new discoveries. Multiplicity across many context tests is not fully controlled, and conditioning on screen quality, QC status, or co-genotypes may induce correlated-context or collider-like dependence. Therefore these conclusions remain dataset-specific associations, not causal claims or claims of external generalizability."
  ,
  "research_notes": "ITER9 appraise priorities. New due evidence assessed. PTEN-loss: overall association already validated. New clean triple-negative co-genotype-free context H159 accepted +0.252 [0.169,0.335] and requested validation as a borderline PTEN refinement. Lower-screen-quality PTEN H165 accepted +0.363 [0.192,0.535] as context support and closed. Broad high-screen-quality PTEN H164 unresolved; matched RNA+DNA PTEN H166 unresolved (lower 0.138); triple-negative high-quality PTEN H167 unresolved. These are precision/context issues, not absence. CDKN2A-loss remains unresolved and not threshold-established: H162 +0.144 [0.083,0.204], H170 +0.144 [0.056,0.231], H168 +0.212 [0.053,0.370], H169 +0.142 [0.053,0.230], H171 +0.159 [0.086,0.232], H163 +0.060 [-0.099,0.219]. Point estimates are directionally compatible but smaller and less stable than central predictors; defer CDKN2A unless a new analysis has strong reason. Technical Avana library H174 rejected +0.053 [-0.035,0.141]; interval entirely below threshold. Central validated map remains: TP53 loss and KEAP1 loss higher dependency_composite/weaker dependency; PTEN loss, SMARCA4 loss, MSI-high lower dependency_composite/stronger dependency. Validation request: H159 only. If H159 validates, accept refined PTEN-loss triple-negative context; if validation is inconclusive but positive, retain discovery acceptance and overall PTEN map; if validation is negative, downgrade the refined context but do not overturn central PTEN unless new direct evidence contradicts it. Avoid overvalidating accepted central contexts. All conclusions are dataset-specific associations; screen-quality, QC, library, ploidy/aneuploidy, and co-genotype conditioning can introduce confounding or collider-like dependence and do not establish causality or generalizability."
